0..76 Comment "//! The Rust extern `f(x: Option<&u32>, n: u32) -> u32` as a function row of"
77..140 Comment "//! the foreign boundary, admitted against its witness records."
141..144 Comment "//!"
145..218 Comment "//! A function type means a Hoare triple over its arguments\' and result\'s"
219..298 Comment "//! realisers. `Option<&u32>` is realised by the null niche, so `f`\'s triple is"
299..373 Comment "//! `deref_or`\'s at `u32`. Each argument\'s and the result\'s witness record"
374..447 Comment "//! summarises its realisers, read off its code by Rust\'s default layout."
448..451 Comment "//!"
452..528 Comment "//! `admit(declared, row)` compares rustc\'s printed layouts with the records"
529..609 Comment "//! clause by clause, answering `Admitted` or `Unrealised` with the first clause"
610..683 Comment "//! that fails. Each extern below differs from the row in one clause. The"
684..758 Comment "//! checker holds the layout program\'s integer comparisons as neutrals, so"
759..787 Comment "//! admission is a run here."
788..791 Comment "//!"
792..874 Comment "//! - see: `ledger/diagnostics/BoundaryUnrealised.Niche.gandr` — an extern block"
875..956 Comment "//!   refused at check time by the clause it fails, `BoundaryUnrealised.<clause>`"
957..1036 Comment "//! - see: `pathological/interop/boundary/function-row.gandr` — the predicate"
1037..1089 Comment "//!   chosen through the layout, refused `Undecided`"
1090..1165 Comment "//! - see: `model/specification/niche-realizability.gandr` — `deref_or`\'s"
1166..1214 Comment "//!   triple and the tagged layout\'s equivalence"
1215..1294 Comment "//! - see: `library/std/src/boundary.gandr` — the row, the witness record and"
1295..1308 Comment "//!   `admit`"
1309..1376 Comment "//! - see: `library/std/src/layout.gandr` — Rust\'s default layout"
1377..1458 Comment "//! - cites: [Wagner-2024-realistic_realizability_specifying_abis_you_can_count],"
1459..1482 Comment "//!   § 3.1 and § 4.2"
1483..1486 Comment "//!"
1487..1678 Comment "//! [Wagner-2024-realistic_realizability_specifying_abis_you_can_count]: https://doi.org/10.1145/3689755 \"Wagner, Eisbach and Ahmed, Realistic Realizability: Specifying ABIs You Can Count On\""
1680..1754 Comment "// The Rust side, built by rustc 1.98.0-nightly (f28ac764c 2026-06-23) for"
1755..1826 Comment "// x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu. Its layouts are from `rustc --edition 2024"
1827..1895 Comment "// --crate-type=lib -Zprint-type-sizes` over the eight externs, with"
1896..1952 Comment "// `size_of`, `align_of` and `needs_drop` for each type:"
1953..1955 Comment "//"
1956..1983 Comment "//     #[unsafe(no_mangle)]"
1984..2044 Comment "//     pub extern \"C\" fn f(x: Option<&u32>, n: u32) -> u32 {"
2045..2092 Comment "//         match x { Some(r) => *r, None => n }"
2093..2101 Comment "//     }"
2102..2104 Comment "//"
2105..2172 Comment "//     type              size  alignment  discriminant   needs drop"
2173..2232 Comment "//     Option<&u32>      8     8          none (niche)   no"
2233..2292 Comment "//     u32               4     4          -              no"
2293..2352 Comment "//     u64               8     8          -              no"
2353..2412 Comment "//     &u32              8     8          -              no"
2413..2472 Comment "//     Tagged            16    8          -              no"
2473..2533 Comment "//     Option<Box<u32>>  8     8          none (niche)   yes"
2534..2593 Comment "//     Packed            4     1          -              no"
2594..2596 Comment "//"
2597..2675 Comment "// `Tagged` is `#[repr(C)] struct Tagged { tag: usize, ptr: *const u32 }`, the"
2676..2739 Comment "// tagged layout of `Option<&u32>` written out, and `Packed` is"
2740..2816 Comment "// `#[repr(C, packed)] struct Packed { v: u32 }`. The externs other than `f`"
2817..2889 Comment "// each differ from the row in one clause: `g` takes `n: u64`, `a` takes"
2890..2959 Comment "// `n: Packed`, `h` takes `x: &u32`, `t` takes `x: Tagged`, `b` takes"
2960..3028 Comment "// `x: Option<Box<u32>>`, `w` answers a `u64`, and `k` takes no `n`."
3030..3039 Comment "// `Nat`."
